tool_input.command:
cd /private/var/folders/mc/rj708cb13vv9x7hm9rptcwqc0000gn/T/p3-6upoklyp; mkdir -p drafts review-records
cat > drafts/0002-quiz.json <<'EOF'
{
  "id": "0002",
  "objective": "O4",
  "title": "Workflows, agents and who owns the control flow",
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "mcq",
      "objective": "O4",
      "stem": "A program gives the model a goal and three tools, runs whichever tool the model asks for, and stops when the model writes a final answer. Who owns the control flow here?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "The program, because the developer fixed the order of steps in advance"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "The model, because it picks the next step each time round the loop"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "Both equally, because the program runs every tool the model requests"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "The tools, because their results decide what happens after each step"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "mcq",
      "objective": "O4",
      "stem": "Booking a slot in your makerspace assistant always goes: check the calendar, write the booking, send a confirmation. Which design fits this part best?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "An agent, so that the model can choose the order of the steps each time"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "An agent, because booking needs tools and only agents can call tools"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "A fixed workflow, because the steps and their order are known in advance"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "A fixed workflow, because a workflow never has to call a language model"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "type": "numeric",
      "objective": "O4",
      "stem": "A fixed workflow handles each booking in 3 model calls. An agent handles the same booking in 7 model calls on average. If every call costs $0.002, how many dollars more does the agent cost for 500 bookings?",
      "unit": "dollars"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "type": "mcq",
      "objective": "O4",
      "stem": "A student's manual question needs one lookup or four, and you cannot tell which from the question. Which design gets the answer right without wasting model calls?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "A fixed workflow that always makes four lookups for every question"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "A fixed workflow that always makes one lookup for every question"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "A fixed workflow that always makes two lookups as a compromise"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "An agent loop with a step limit, where the model decides when it has enough"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "type": "short",
      "objective": "O4",
      "stem": "Your code asks the model to label each question \"manual\" or \"booking\", then runs a fixed manual path or a fixed booking path. Who still owns the control flow: the model or the code?"
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "type": "mcq",
      "objective": "O4",
      "stem": "Your sponsor wants a firm limit on the cost of each booking. Why does a fixed workflow suit that better than an agent?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "Its number of model calls is set in advance, so the cost is predictable"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "It always uses a cheaper model than an agent does, whatever the task"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "It makes no tool calls, so no tool costs can ever be added to the bill"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "An agent has no way to be stopped, so its cost cannot be limited"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
EOF
cat > drafts/0002-key.json <<'EOF'
{
  "id": "0002",
  "items": [
    {"id": "q1", "answer": "b", "rationale": "In an agent the model decides the next step each time round the loop; the program only runs the tools. Running the tools is not the same as owning the control flow."},
    {"id": "q2", "answer": "c", "rationale": "When the steps and their order are known in advance, code can fix them, which is a workflow. Workflows can call tools and models too; the difference is who picks the next step."},
    {"id": "q3", "answer": 4, "tolerance": 0.05, "rationale": "The agent uses 7 - 3 = 4 extra calls per booking: 4 x $0.002 = $0.008, and 500 bookings x $0.008 = $4."},
    {"id": "q4", "answer": "d", "rationale": "When the path cannot be known in advance, the model has to choose how many lookups to make, so an agent with a step limit fits. Any fixed count either wastes calls or misses information."},
    {"id": "q5", "answer": "the code", "accepted": ["code", "the program", "program", "the program's code", "the developer", "the code owns it", "the code, not the model"], "rationale": "The model only fills in one step (the label); the code fixes which path runs next, so this is still a workflow. The model owns the control flow only when it chooses the steps itself."},
    {"id": "q6", "answer": "a", "rationale": "A workflow's steps, and so its number of model calls, are fixed in advance, so cost per request is known. Agents can be stopped with a step limit, but their number of calls varies."}
  ]
}
EOF
